# Target C: a cap forces a diameter separator, and interior sites can pay its cut

**Date:** 2026-10-06. **Authority:** main
`583203ac1dc1d7bc49f0ca0574d75e85f9de2ee1`, state
`2026-10-05.audit.62`. **Worker:** 2, actual macroscopic exterior caps.
**Classification:** proved candidates awaiting independent audit for the
separator and motion; regression/discovery theorem for the explicit refill.
Repository lifecycle remains unreviewed. C-II and Level I remain open.
No external literature or unpromoted Worker 1 theorem is used.

## 1. The new composition and its limit

The previous unloaded stress cut moved every actual diameter but supplied
no locations for the deleted labels. Here the actual cap chord gives a
geometric cut, without a stress-space selection:

```text
actual macroscopic cap + actual diameter partners
  -> loaded endpoints avoid a line through the source center
  -> every actual diameter crosses that line; H is a forest
  -> delete only one shore's contact guards in a width-one strip
  -> translate the retained core shores inward, keeping all other contacts
  -> strict reduced-source diameter, with every original isolate retained
  -> enough interior replacement sites (not forced in canonical sources).
```

There is also a fixed-width full-source strip version with a uniform finite
translation. Its deletion cost is O(D), including the removed isolates.
Both versions move the old diameter-carrying core. Neither claims that
strict-safe area supplies a site.

The explicit family in Section 5 does restore cardinality: two interior
sites, each anchored by two exact unit contacts, replace two unloaded seam
guards after a finite core translation. All actual old diameters disappear.
It even admits a triangle-free strict-sub-D endpoint. This is an actual
cap/core packet mechanism, but the family's deficient core prevents an
inference about canonical sources. The missing statement is a **forced
interior packet after the cap-induced cut**, specified in Section 6.

## 2. A finite loaded-endpoint separator from one actual cap

Translate the full-source circumcenter to zero. Let R be its circumradius,
and suppose every actual diameter midpoint has norm at most L. Canonical
FS.1/FS.6 allow

\[
 R<D/2+\eta,\qquad L=\sqrt{D\eta+\eta^2},\qquad
 \eta=3^{1/4}\sqrt D.                                      \tag{1}
\]

Let an actual exterior cap have closing line u dot x=c, where |u|=1,
F lies in u dot x<=c, and c>=0. Put w=Ju and r=D-R. Assume

\[
 c+2L<r,\qquad
 g:=\sqrt{r^2-(c+2L)^2}>L.                                \tag{2}
\]

**Theorem S.1 (finite cap separator).** Every loaded endpoint b satisfies
|w dot b|>=g, and the endpoints of every actual diameter have opposite
w signs. In particular every actual diameter crosses the line w dot x=0.

**Proof.** A loaded endpoint b has an actual partner k. Since |k|<=R and
|b-k|=D, |b|>=r. Also b,k belong to F, so u dot b,u dot k<=c. The
midpoint bound |b+k|<=2L yields u dot b>=-c-2L. Hence
|u dot b|<=c+2L and

\[
 |w\cdot b|^2=|b|^2-|u\cdot b|^2\ge g^2.                 \tag{3}
\]

If an actual pair b,k had the same w sign, then
|w dot (b+k)|>=2g>2L, impossible. QED.

### Macroscopic application

For a cap sequence with area(C)>=alpha D^2, alpha>0 fixed, (2) holds for
all sufficiently large D, and g>=gamma_alpha D for some gamma_alpha>0.
Indeed C lies in the radius-R disk above its closing line. Since R/D->1/2,
a sequence c/D->1/2 would have area(C)/D^2->0 by the circular-segment
area formula (or scaled disk convergence). Thus c/D<=1/2-epsilon_alpha
eventually. Since r/D->1/2 and L/D->0, (2)--(3) give the claimed linear
gap. Canonical DB.30 ensures c>0. No exact central symmetry is assumed.

**Corollary S.2.** For every sufficiently large canonical source with a
macroscopic exterior cap, its **full actual diameter graph is in the
Woodall forest branch**: d_H=q-h>0, and its components are caterpillars.

The separator makes H bipartite. Canonical C-WOODALL says the only other
possibility is one odd-unicyclic component with pendant leaves, which is
not bipartite. This rules out that alternative. It does not exclude the
nonmatching forest. Canonical C-MATCHING-EXCLUSION only excludes a forest
made entirely of isolated matching edges.

The branch is also quantitatively nonterminal. If A_C is the source-hull
arc length of the cap between its loaded endpoints, split partial(K)
into the q consecutive loaded arcs. Every other arc has length at least
one, so

\[
 q\le\operatorname{per}(K)-A_C+1\le\pi D-A_C+1.          \tag{3a}
\]

A macroscopic cap has A_C=Omega(D). Thus q/D is bounded away from pi
by a fixed positive amount. Worker 1's terminal O--O depletion, which
assumes q/D->pi, cannot be applied to this exterior-cap sequence.

The argument uses one actual closing chord and its endpoints' actual
partners. The audited proof-only antipodal pairing is consistent with it,
but is not a premise: the opposite slab restriction follows directly
from the actual partners. The line passes through the two limiting cap
middles and misses every loaded endpoint by Omega(D).

## 3. Delete a width-one contact cut and preserve the remaining contacts

Use S.1, with g>1. Partition the original positive-contact core as

\[
 V_-=\{p\in V:w\cdot p\le0\},\qquad
 V_+=\{p\in V:w\cdot p>0\}.
\]

Let E_cross be the old unit contacts joining these shores, and let

\[
 R_-:=\{p\in V_-:\exists q\in V_+,\ pq\in C\}.            \tag{4}
\]

Thus every cross contact is deleted by removing its negative-shore end.
Every point of R_- has -1<w dot p<=0. No loaded endpoint belongs to R_-,
because all of them have projection magnitude at least g>1.

**Theorem S.3 (cap-induced contact cut).** The set R_- is nonempty,
consists only of unloaded core labels, and satisfies

\[
 |R_-|\le (1/a_0+1)D+2,
 \qquad a_0=\sqrt3/2.                                    \tag{5}
\]

There is a sufficiently small epsilon>0 for which the motion

\[
 p\mapsto p+\epsilon w\ (p\in V_-\setminus R_-),\qquad
 p\mapsto p-\epsilon w\ (p\in V_+),                       \tag{6}
\]

with **every original contact isolate fixed**, has all the following
properties:

- every surviving old contact stays exactly unit;
- every old actual diameter remains present and becomes strictly shorter;
- every other full-source lower/upper constraint is checked and preserved;
- the resulting (n-|R_-|)-point source has diameter strictly below D.

**Proof.** The connected core contains loaded endpoints on both shores,
so it has a cross contact. This proves nonemptiness. The width-one
rectangle containing R_- has tangential span at most D. Its area is at
most D and perimeter at most 2D+2. Oler applied to conv(R_-) gives (5),
including the harmless relaxed constant 2. The singleton case also
satisfies (5).

After (4) no surviving contact crosses the shores. Every surviving
contact's endpoints therefore receive the same translation in (6).
For an old diameter with negative endpoint b and positive endpoint k,
let d=w dot (k-b)>=2g. Its exact new squared length is

\[
 D^2-4\epsilon d+4\epsilon^2<D^2
 \quad(0<\epsilon<2g).                                  \tag{7}
\]

All such endpoints are retained. No old diameter lies within one shore.
For every originally strict lower pair, and every originally strict
upper pair, take its positive distance margin. Pairs involving isolates
have both margins because isolates have neither contacts nor diameters.
The finite minimum of the relevant strict margins is positive. Choose
epsilon smaller than one fourth of that minimum and smaller than g.
Each moving-pair distance changes by at most 2epsilon, so all these
strict inequalities persist. Isolate-isolate distances are unchanged.
This proves finite feasibility and strict upper distances for every pair.
It introduces no new contact. QED.

This is an actual motion of core coordinates, not a choice of independent
train owners. Its amplitude has **no uniform lower bound**: retained
isolates can make the finite margin choice small. The deletion set is
geometrically localized, unlike the earlier abstract <=s_Z+1 transversal.
Both cuts are valid; neither controls the other's cardinality.

The contact cut is itself a stress transversal. The stress-work identity
on the reduced source would equate zero contact work with strictly
negative diameter work along (6). Normalization makes the total diameter
weight positive; virial excludes a nonzero contact-only stress. This
observation supplies no insertion credit.

### Localized small stress cuts and an exact aggregate force bound

The same separator argument works at every parallel line w dot x=t,
where -g+1<t<g-1. Let R_t be the negative-shore ends of its cross
contacts. Then R_t lies in the width-one strip t-1<w dot x<=t and
contains no loaded point. All old diameters still cross this cut.

**Theorem S.3-L.** Each R_t contains an unloaded stress transversal
U_t with |U_t|<=s_Z+1, where s_Z is the canonical affine dimension of
the normalized KKT polytope. There are Omega(D) pairwise disjoint such
transversals if the cut parameters are chosen two units apart. Moreover,
for **every** normalized nonnegative KKT stress,

\[
 \sum_{e\in C\text{ crossing }t}\alpha_e\,d_e
 =\sum_{h\in H}\beta_h\,d_h\ge2g,
 \qquad d_e=w\cdot(q_e-p_e)>0,                           \tag{7a}
\]

where each pair is ordered from the negative to the positive shore.
In particular sum_cross alpha_e>=2g, since d_e<=1 for a unit contact.

**Proof.** Give the original core the step velocity +w on its negative
shore and -w on its positive shore, including the labels to be deleted.
Internal contact work is zero. A cross contact's half-squared-distance
derivative is -2d_e; an actual diameter's is -2d_h. Pairing the KKT
equilibrium with this velocity gives the equality in (7a). Every
diameter span is at least 2g and sum beta=1, proving the inequality.
Thus no normalized stress can vanish on all contacts incident to R_t.

For each u in R_t use the zero face F_u of its incident contact load.
Their intersection is empty by (7a). In a minimal empty subfamily U_t,
choose, for each u, a stress belonging to all other selected faces.
Its incident load at u is positive. These witness stresses are affinely
independent: apply each incident-load functional to an affine dependence
to isolate its one coefficient. Hence |U_t|<=dim_aff(P)+1=s_Z+1,
using canonical Section 64.1. Each U_t is nonempty and destroying its
incident contacts removes every normalized KKT stress. The signed-row
separation proof in the earlier E.5 then supplies strict reduced-source
descent after deleting U_t, retaining every isolate. Width-one strips
at two-unit-separated t are disjoint, and the interval of available
t has length 2g-2=Omega(D), proving the disjointness assertion. QED.

These small localized cuts generally require E.5's full core motion;
deleting only U_t does not guarantee the two rigid-shore motion (6).
Equation (7a) is an **aggregate** lower force bound on a physical cut,
not a lower bound on individual KKT coefficients. Neither localization
nor the many disjoint choices implies packet capacity or simultaneous
independent motions. No owner or train argument is being assumed.

## 4. A uniform finite translation after a full-source strip deletion

For 1/2<a<g put R_a={p in A:|w dot p|<a}. Delete **all** these labels,
including the actual isolates in the two caps, and move each remaining
negative/positive source shore by +/-epsilon w, where

\[
 0<\epsilon<a-1/2.                                      \tag{8}
\]

**Theorem S.4.** This produces a unit-separated (n-|R_a|)-point source
of diameter <D and removes only

\[
 |R_a|\le (2a/a_0+1)D+2a+1                             \tag{9}
\]

labels. All actual diameter endpoints are retained.

**Proof.** Within either shore every distance is unchanged. Across the
shores the new normal separation is at least 2(a-epsilon)>1. If d is
the old normal separation, d>=2a, the squared length changes by
-4epsilon d+4epsilon^2<=-4epsilon(2a-epsilon)<0. Every cross upper
distance decreases. A same-shore pair is strictly shorter than D because
S.1 puts every old actual diameter across the shores. Thus the full
endpoint is strict-sub-D. The width-2a, length-at-most-D rectangle gives
(9) by Oler. QED.

If D_-,D_+ are the two old shore diameters, an explicit bound is

\[
 D_{new}\le\max\{D_-,D_+,\sqrt{D^2-4\epsilon(2a-\epsilon)}\}<D. \tag{10}
\]

For a=1, epsilon=1/4, all cross lower projections are at least 3/2,
and their squared lengths drop by at least 7/4. Every old actual
diameter has the stronger drop 2g-1/4=Omega(D). Same-shore inactive
pairs may still control (10); no uniform full-source diameter decrease
is claimed. Each macroscopic cap loses only O(D) source labels, so
both retain their quadratic populations. This is a uniform finite
**reduced-cardinality** motion, not a global competitor.

## 5. An exact interior refill after the actual diameter core moves

Reuse only the explicitly defined nonglobal spoke family from
`target_c_paired_cap_spoke_global_comparison_2026-10-06.md`. The finite
packet below is different from that report's independent disk competitor.

In triangular basis e1=(1,0), e2=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2), let the six unit rays
be v_j, j=0,...,5. For integer M>=20 the core is
T_M={k v_j:1<=k<=M}. It has six unit spokes and a central unit hexagon.
Its only actual diameters are the three pairs +/-M v_j, with D=2M.
Every actual core contact and diameter has the positive KKT weights
alpha=2M/3 and beta=1/3. Let

\[
 s=1+1/(100M^2),\quad R_0=M-1/4,
\]

and retain the cloud sI_M, where I_M consists of the lattice sites in
B(0,R_0) whose lattice distance from T_M is greater than one.
All these cloud points are actual contact isolates. The six actual
loaded-hull caps are macroscopic, pair antipodally, have boundary-order
slack, and satisfy the previously proved deep-cap coverage.

Take w=e2-e1, so w dot (i e1+j e2)=(j-i)/2. The only cross contacts
are v0-v1 and v3-v4. S.3's cut is precisely

\[
 R_- =\{v_0,v_4\}=\{(1,0),(0,-1)\}.                      \tag{11}
\]

Delete them and use (6) with epsilon=1/10, keeping **all** cloud points
fixed. Insert just two sites

\[
 y_k=(k+1)e_1-e_2+\epsilon w,\qquad k\in\{3,5\}.         \tag{12}
\]

Each has exact unit contacts to the retained, translated pair
k e1,(k+1)e1. Each lies deep inside the old force hull. They are
interior guard sites, not sites in either exterior cap or its base.

**Theorem S.5 (parametric discovery exchange).** For every integer
M>=20, (11)--(12) produces a same-cardinality unit-separated endpoint
with exact diameter

\[
 D_{new}=\sqrt{4M^2-(2/5)M+1/25}<2M.                      \tag{13}
\]

Six old contacts are lost and four new ones are created. The initial
packet creates two unit triangles; these are allowed by the strict
diameter comparison. Alternatively both new sites can be moved a
sufficiently small common distance vertically downward to remove all
four new contacts, giving a triangle-free strict-sub-D endpoint.

**Proof of all lower constraints.** Within a core shore all old
distances remain unchanged. After deleting the two cross contacts,
the smallest old cross-core distance is at least sqrt(3), since
lattice squared lengths are integers and all unit cross pairs were
deleted. A displacement of at most 1/5 leaves these distances >1.
Each core-cloud distance is at least sqrt(3)-1/(100M)-1/10>1.
Cloud-cloud distances are unchanged and strict.

For a site q_k=(k+1,-1), its only unit core neighbors are k e1 and
(k+1)e1; they move with it. All other same-shore core distances were
at least sqrt(3). Its normal separation from every opposite-shore
core point remains greater than 3, so those pairs are strict too.
The two inserted sites are distance 2 apart.

Any cloud lattice index m with |q_k-m|>=sqrt(3) satisfies
|y_k-sm|>=sqrt(3)-1/10-1/(100M)>1. Among q_k's six lattice unit
neighbors, only m=(k+1,-2) and m=(k+2,-2) can be non-guards. For
these q_k-m is respectively e2 or w, so

\[
 |q_k-m+\epsilon w|^2\ge1+\epsilon+\epsilon^2=111/100.
\]

Scaling m changes this distance by at most 1/(100M), giving a
strict lower distance >1. This checks every old isolate, including
any near-unit inactive blocker at (12).

**Proof of all upper constraints.** The largest retained non-diameter
core distance was at most 2M-1; shore translations increase any such
distance by at most 1/5. The two old diameters in directions v0,v1
now have squared length 4M^2-(2/5)M+1/25; the third has smaller
squared length 4M^2-(4/5)M+1/25. Thus the largest core distance is
(13). A moved core point has radius at most M+1/10; a cloud point
has radius at most M-1/4+1/(100M). Every core-cloud distance is
therefore at most 2M-3/20+1/(100M)<2M-7/50<D_new. Cloud-cloud
distances are smaller still. A new site has radius below 6, giving
all its distances to the remaining source below M+61/10<2M-7/50
for M>=20. The two new sites are distance 2 apart. Every upper
pair has now been checked.

Each deleted core vertex has degree three, and they are nonadjacent,
so old contact loss is six. The only new contacts are the four specified
ones. For the triangle-free endpoint, the vectors from either new
site to its two core anchors have negative vertical component. A
common sufficiently small downward translation strictly opens these
four contacts. All other involving lower and upper distances have
positive finite margins. They persist; no other contact changes. QED.

The proof is analytic for all M>=20. The exact verifier checks full
M=20 and M=32 endpoints with two independent integer squared-distance
formulas. It also chooses and verifies a rational finite downward step;
there is no reliance on an infinitesimal assertion. The complete M=20
old source, initial packet endpoint, and triangle-free endpoint are saved.

### Why this is still a regression, not cap closure

This family has h=3 and q=6 for all M. Its e+h=6M+3 is below the
canonical requirement 2v-2=12M-2. It fails canonical mixed rigidity,
the affine core kernel, the linear loaded/diameter populations, and
the unloaded-degree requirement. Positive KKT, cap pairing, near-disk
geometry and isolate saturation do not repair these missing hypotheses.
The packet proves an actual finite cap/core release in the stated family;
it is not forced in a canonical source.

In particular, (12)'s interior guard vacancies are deliberately present
in this one-chart cloud. Canonical density and near-unit matching do not
supply such vacancies. No strict-area or generic Oler estimate is used
to assert that they exist in an actual survivor.

### Exact failure of a proposed extension to arbitrary core edges

One tempting next inference would be that every same-shore unit core
edge provides an equilateral-completion site like (12) after (6).
An actual strict unit rhombus already defeats that inference. Put

\[
 a=(0,0),\quad b=(1,0),\quad
 d=(3/7,2\sqrt{10}/7),\quad c=b+d.
\]

All four boundary edges have length one; the squared diagonal lengths
are 8/7 and 20/7, both greater than one. This is a unit-separated,
unit-triangle-free strict rhombus, an allowed canonical physical-face
geometry. Its equilateral completion of ab into the face is
q=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2), and

\[
 |q-d|^2=(11-2\sqrt{30})/7\in(0,1).                      \tag{14}
\]

If the entire face belongs to one shore, its core translation and
the corresponding completion-site translation leave (14) unchanged.
The completion is blocked by a retained **core** point, rather than
an exterior isolate that the motion might clear. More generally a
unit-rhombus angle theta in (pi/3,pi/2] puts that other unit neighbor
within 2sin((theta-pi/3)/2)<=2sin(pi/12)<1 of the completion.

This is a regression against the edge-to-vacancy inference, not against
S.1--S.5 or the universal cap theorem. It does not assert a complete
canonical/global source embedding. The exact certificate records the
rhombus and (14). Any forced packet proof must distinguish actual
outer-face interior pockets from these occupied rhombic completions;
unit-contact density alone supplies no sites.

## 6. The exact stronger packet obligation

For a canonical macroscopic-cap source choose S.1's separator. Either
use the unloaded contact cut R_- and its feasible endpoint A_epsilon,
or the full-source strip R_a and endpoint A_{a,epsilon}. Let r be
the exact number of deleted labels. A sufficient finite closing lemma is:

> At one of these actual feasible endpoints there is a unit-separated
> packet Y of at least r sites, unit-clear from every retained source
> point, with all pairs involving Y strictly below the original D.

Selecting exactly r sites gives the same original cardinality and
diameter <D. The packet may contain triangles and use int(F); all
original source constraints must be checked. Further movement of the
retained source is allowed in the broader nonlocal-refill obligation,
but is not supplied by S.1--S.4. The stated fixed-endpoint packet
lemma is sufficient, not claimed necessary for every possible cap route.

The newly explicit cut and motion eliminate two earlier ambiguities:
which actual diameter endpoints move, and which removed labels must be
restored. S.5 shows that exact-contact interior packets can pay those
labels after a finite move. What remains is **forcing enough such
sites in the canonical source**, where the defective spoke guards are
unavailable. No uniform motion amplitude is claimed for S.3, and no
packet credit follows from S.4's uniform motion or remaining cap area.

S.3-L additionally permits the <=s_Z+1 deletion set to be selected inside
any one of linearly many disjoint width-one strips. This localizes the
abstract nonlocal-refill problem. It does not prove a fixed-endpoint
packet for either the full shore cut or that smaller transversal.

The old exterior packet release E.1/E.6 applies with the old core fixed.
It neither supplies nor rules out (12) after the core moves. The missing
canonical packet must still cross into the core region or otherwise use
the actual moved geometry. The exact-contact sites in S.5 are an explicit
example of that distinction.

## 7. Regression and coordination audit

| Binding condition | Treatment |
| --- | --- |
| Strained triangular cap / no spare strict site | No cap spare inferred; S.5 uses explicit interior guard sites |
| Adaptive strict-safe saturation | Fully retained; only moved-source packet sites are used |
| Old diameter core fixed | Both actual loaded shores move; every H edge is checked |
| KKT invisible isolate mass | All isolates retained/fixed in S.3 and S.5; fully counted in S.4 |
| Approximate antipodality | S.1 uses the finite midpoint bound; no exact symmetry is imposed |
| Boundary-order cap slack | No slack threshold or scalar capacity reversal is asserted |
| Inactive near-pairs | Finite margins in S.3; uniform separation in S.4; exact complete checks in S.5 |
| Unit triangle in a competitor | Permitted because full diameter is strictly smaller; a triangle-free endpoint is also provided |
| Canonical mixed rigidity / h density | Explicitly absent in S.5; no universal refill conclusion |

